<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sonargaon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Suvarnagrama</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sonargaon, the old Suvarnagrama, is a historic town near Narayanganj in central Bangladesh. It was a capital of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> under the Deva dynasty and then of Muslim governors and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakhruddin-mubarak-shah">Fakhruddin Mubarak Shah</a> in the 14th century. Visitors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> described its trade, and in the late 16th century it was the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-khan">Isa Khan</a>, leader of the Baro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuyans</a>. Its ruined merchant quarter, Panam Nagar, survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sonargaon.jpg" alt="Sonargaon" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rayhan9d · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%A6%97%E0%A7%8B%E0%A6%AF%E0%A6%BC%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%B2%E0%A6%A6%E0%A6%BF_%E0%A6%AE%E0%A6%B8%E0%A6%9C%E0%A6%BF%E0%A6%A6_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
